v0.13.0 — the release where a turn stops being all-or-nothing. Pressing Enter while the model is streaming no longer forces a choice between waiting out a ten-minute tool chain and throwing away the results already computed: the message is queued and delivered at the next legal reasoning boundary. On the machine side, guidance speaks only on failure *shapes* — a retry loop, a run of failed calls — never on success, and never in a way that extends the turn. (The third rule designed alongside them, silence-run, was killed by replaying 390 real sessions: it fired in 70% of them.) eval grows its third layer: an LLM-as-judge over final_text plus external anchors, calibrated on a real lane to 0.88 agreement with zero false passes — the gate stays human-opened. The web face goes from usable to tidy, and the subtraction discipline takes three more cuts.

Notable changes:

- **Mid-stream user messages (new behaviour).** `QueueUserMessage` pushes text into a locked queue that the agent loop drains at exactly two boundaries: the loop head of turn > 0 (tool results are already in history, assistant `tool_calls` and their results are still adjacent, so no 400), and the settlement point where the model wants to close (an about-to-return becomes one extra turn instead of being silently dropped). Limits are rejected at enqueue — 8KB per message, 4 per turn — never silently truncated, because injected text is permanent history and is re-sent on every subsequent request. The TUI side is an optional sibling interface (`midTurnInjector`), so every fake agent in tests falls back to the old path untouched; on success it prints "⤵ inserted at the next boundary" and writes the injected block back into scrollback so the user can see where their words landed. When rejected, the queued/steer paths run byte-for-byte as before. `Esc` clears the queue — leftovers never resurrect across turns.

- **Turn-boundary guidance (new package `internal/guidance`).** Deterministic, model-free rules that fire only on failure shapes: `retry-loop` (the same name plus normalized args failing three times in a row — the previous failure is thousands of tokens back and each result looks reasonable in isolation) and `failure-run` (three consecutive failed tool calls, possibly of different tools — the pattern is invisible inside any single result). Three hard constraints: nothing on turn 0, a human interjection always wins the boundary, and guidance only ever rides the loop head, never the settlement point — a machine hint must never buy itself a turn. The evidence is direct: successful-path `[hint:]` lines fired 3,871 times across 40 sessions for a 0.6pp real change in the next action. A hint needs an audience; once the action is done, a hint is just commentary.

- **`seek ui-trace` (new).** Injections, queuing, steering, withdraw and Esc are same-shaped or shapeless in the transcript: an injection and a steered resend land as byte-identical `user` lines, a withdraw writes nothing, a cancelled turn leaves at most an orphan `tool_call`. None of it can be folded from any transcript, so `~/.seek/ui-trace.jsonl` records the facts (interaction kind, delivery, rule name) and `seek ui-trace` reports on them — same reasoning as `sessmeta`: facts on disk, conclusions computed.

- **eval v3 — the judge layer (J1/J2/J3).** `J1`: an LLM-as-judge over `final_text`, opt-in and record-only until calibrated. `J2`: a calibration set plus an agreement report — five real-lane iterations over 34 items closed at 0.88 agreement, false-pass = 0, gate-eligible (kimi-k3.1-pro on the OpenCode Go subscription lane; the credential path is login passthrough and is recorded in the report). `J3`: the first scored cases wired in. Hardening that came out of the real runs: judge verdict parsing now handles two streaming shapes, two anchor crash forms are fixed (check stdout never re-enters the metrics stream, binary paths normalize to absolute), and `judge-calibrate` splits "lane outage" from "bad quality" so a broken lane cannot masquerade as a judgment.

- **eval v3 — external anchors (A1/A2/A3).** A private terminal-bench-lite task set of 8 items with its own lane, mechanism self-tests and a cross-harness comparison protocol — anchors that do not come from our own eval corpus.

- **The web face, tidied.** Icons and buttons unified: every emoji and text-glyph icon replaced by an inline Lucide sprite (vendored paths, zero runtime dependency — CSP `default-src 'self'`, single-file, no build step), buttons collapsed into three tiers. 390px phone width: single-column drawer, wrapping button groups, stacked settings. Stats grow from a per-session snapshot to a cross-session trend (`stats/trend` + hand-drawn SVG, per-session cost column in the sidebar). `/bind` gains a project picker and a directory browser (new read-only `fs/list`, roots narrowed to `serve.project_roots` ∪ configured projects ∪ memory roots). Markdown rendering completed: GFM tables, blockquotes, paragraphs, code-block containers, list continuation. Dark-mode secondary text lifted to primary (3.59/2.29 → ≥6.0). Copy and panel details: short timestamps, 「N 轮」, main-area empty states, disabled send button, statistics rhythm.

- **Tools and lanes.** bash/grep successful-path hints cut by 67% after measurement showed no effect, redundant `cd` prefixes stripped, and grep gains five shell-equivalent parameters (`-v`, `-l`, `-c`, and friends). `ask_user` on headless lanes now returns `ErrDisabled` — "interactive question not available in this mode, rephrase as a plain-text question" — instead of `ErrNoCallback: programming error`: the default inverted (`askPolicy` is born Disabled; the TUI explicitly enables Ask at its own entrance), because every headless lane registers the tool and none has a callback, and a model told "you made a programming error" just retries the same call. `-no-save` mints an ephemeral session id for the routing header only, fixing the OpenCode Go login-lane headless 400 (`MissingSessionID`) that the judge smoke test caught. The sysprompt identity block is corrected and condensed: stale size figures dropped, GLM added to the provider list, nine lines folded into five.

- **Three subtraction knives.** `fim_complete` retires end to end — the tool package, the FIM client in `pkg/deepseek`, its sysprompt documentation line, its D8 deepseekOnly entry, the benchmark FIM metrics — on measured usage of 0%/11%: the model does not use the fast path, so keeping it costs more than deleting it. `think` retires with the `dual-model` built-in skill and the whole deepSeekOnly filter chain behind it (two calls across 109 real project sessions; depth now rides the main session's thinking mode). The sixth deadcode knife clears the first batch of class B — dead from production's view, alive only because tests reference it — each item decided semantically, with a double-mode `deadcode` re-run and a repo-wide grep before the cut. Same standard for all three: unused is not free.

- **Engineering and gates.** The merge gates settle on doc-budgets / pitfalls-count / web-gate (selftest describes them as measurement-line gates; cards mark `not-in-lane` and the merge point runs them). `.butler/merge-gates.json` leaves version control — the gate declaration is a machine-local file. The web review evidence pack gains visual trend counts and `web-page-review.sh` becomes the headless-screenshot review entrance. The pitfalls ledger keeps splitting into one file per entry.

Prefix cache: the bash and grep tool descriptions changed in this release, so the first session after upgrading recomputes the prompt prefix once; hits resume from there.
